course: dictionary
lesson: 9614
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one to use. Strong is a good example. A strong man has power in his body, but a strong rope is hard to break. The clues are the words next to it: man, rope, tea, wind.
explain: «Muscles get stronger mostly as each fibre gets thicker.» Here stronger means more powerful, like the body meaning. Try the swap check: put in bigger or more powerful, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «A child walks a big or strong dog only with an adult, because it can pull a child over.» Strong here means it has a lot of power, and that power can be a danger. A strong learner is different again: it means good at learning, not physical power.
fact: Strong is an adjective, so it describes a noun: a strong man, a strong rope.
fact: «Strong» means having a lot of power in your body, or not easy to break or damage.
fact: «Strong» can mean having a big effect, like «strong wind» or «a strong smell», or a lot of flavour, like «strong tea».
fact: «Strong» can mean firm and sure, as in «a strong belief», or good at something, as in «a strong reader».
fact: The forms stronger and strongest are used to compare things: «stronger» for two, «strongest» for three or more.
exercise: «Spider silk is stronger than steel for its weight.» What does stronger mean here? | Hotter and drier | *Harder to break or damage | Lighter in colour
exercise: Which meaning fits «strong tea»? | *With a lot of flavour | Not easy to break | Firm and sure | Having a lot of power in your body
exercise: «She is a strong reader.» What does strong mean? | *Good at reading | Loud | Having a lot of power in her arms | Not easy to break
test: «Curiosity makes you a strong learner.» What does strong mean in this sentence? | *Good at something, very capable | Firm rope | Having a big smell | Having big muscles
test: «A strong wind blew the tent over.» What does strong mean? | Firm and sure | With a lot of flavour | *Having a big effect, very powerful | Not easy to break
exam: Tom says strong only means having big muscles. Is he right? | Yes, but only for dogs | *No, strong has several meanings, such as a strong rope or strong tea | Yes, that is the only meaning | No, it only means slow
